13. What to keep from this optional day
The transferable ideas are more important than the exact thresholds:

Day 4 deliberately integrates one RNA-seq contrast at a time; untreated and treated DESeq2 results are not merged into one table;
this case study uses the untreated WT-versus-ΔHP1021 RNA-seq result together with Day 3 peaks already selected as WT-specific versus ΔHP1021;
Day 2 and Day 3 results can only be integrated safely when they use the same reference and compatible identifiers;
genomic interval operations should be done with interval-aware objects;
nearest ORF, nearest DEG, ORF overlap and promoter overlap answer different questions;
strand changes the location of an upstream promoter window;
a Hits object stores relationships between interval sets;
a peak can be decorated with the statistics of its closest ORF without implying causality;
intragenic / promoter-proximal / intergenic is a useful summary, but detailed one-to-many overlaps should still be retained;
an indexed FASTA can return only the requested sequences;
GC and CpG content are properties of the peak sequences, not evidence of regulation;
WT-specific ChIP-seq binding plus untreated RNA-seq change generates a hypothesis, not a proof.

What you will have ready today
By the end of Day 0, you should be able to:

access GitHub;
access Docker Hub;
install and open Visual Studio Code;
install Git and verify that it works;
install Docker and verify that it can run a container;
install and open IGV.

These steps should be completed before the start of the workshop.
For any problems, questions, or installation issues, follow the issue-reporting instructions at the end of this page.

6. Report a setup problem
If a setup problem remains after checking the table above, email:
francesco.gualdrini@gmail.com
Use this exact email subject:
Issue - Reproducible NGS workshop
In the message, include:

your operating system and version;
the Day 0 step where the problem occurred;
the command you ran;
the complete error message or a screenshot of it;
which readiness checks completed successfully.

Do not include passwords, access tokens, or account recovery codes.

12. Checkpoint route
If mapping or peak calling is too slow on a laptop, continue from checkpoint files generated against GCF_025998455.1 and NZ_AP026446.1.
At minimum, record:

which upstream steps were demonstrated but not executed locally;
which checkpoint files were used;
the reference assembly and annotation versions;
which downstream steps you executed yourself.
